    "content": "A magnitude 5.0–5.9 earthquake is classified as moderate, causing light-to-moderate damage to buildings and structures. While generally not destructive in well-built areas, it can break dishes, knock items off shelves, crack plaster, and shake people awake, with energy equivalent to approximately 1 million pounds of explosive. Key Characteristics of a Magnitude 5 Earthquake: Intensity: Often felt by most people in the vicinity, creating strong shaking. Damage Potential: Can cause slight damage to buildings, particularly weak or poorly constructed structures. Comparison: It is 10 times stronger in terms of shaking amplitude than a magnitude 4.0 and about 32 times more energy release than a magnitude 4.0. Frequency: Occurs frequently worldwide, acting as a \"wake-up call\" in affected areas. What to Expect: Windows, dishes, and doors rattle, with items falling off shelves. Weakened masonry or chimneys may crack or fall. Trees and bushes might shake slightly. Modern, well-built structures typically suffer only minor damage, if any.",
